.TH XUR.STD.NET 7 "2026-10-29" "xur 0.1.2" "Xur Manual"
.SH NAME
xur.std.net \- TCP, UDP and Unix-domain sockets
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B use std::net;
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B std::net
provides networking with a text-oriented interface. All I/O operations
return
.BR Result .
.SH TYPES
.SS TcpListener
.TP
.B TcpListener::bind(address) -> Result<TcpListener, str>
Bind a TCP listener to an address (e.g.\& "127.0.0.1:8080").
.TP
.B listener.accept() -> Result<(TcpStream, str), str>
Accept a connection, returning the stream and the peer address.
.TP
.B listener.local_addr() -> Result<str, str>
The listener's bound address.
.SS TcpStream
.TP
.B TcpStream::connect(address) -> Result<TcpStream, str>
Connect to a TCP server.
.TP
.B stream.write(text)
Write a string to the stream.
.TP
.B stream.read_line() -> Result<str, str>
Read one line from the stream.
.TP
.B stream.read(max) -> Result<str, str>
Read up to
.I max
bytes and return them as a string.
.TP
.B stream.shutdown()
Shut down both halves of the connection.
.SS UdpSocket
.TP
.B UdpSocket::bind(address) -> Result<UdpSocket, str>
Bind a UDP socket.
.TP
.B socket.send_to(text, address)
Send a string as a datagram.
.TP
.B socket.recv_from(max) -> Result<(str, str), str>
Receive a datagram, returning the text and the sender's address.
.TP
.B socket.local_addr() -> Result<str, str>
The socket's bound address.
.SS UnixListener (Unix only)
.TP
.B UnixListener::bind(path) -> Result<UnixListener, str>
Bind a Unix-domain socket.
.TP
.B listener.accept() -> Result<UnixStream, str>
Accept a connection.
.SS UnixStream (Unix only)
.TP
.B UnixStream::connect(path) -> Result<UnixStream, str>
Connect to a Unix-domain socket.
.PP
Unix streams have the same methods as TCP streams:
.BR write ,
.BR read_line ,
.BR read ,
.BR shutdown .
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
use std::net;
use std::io;

fn main() -> Result<(), str> {
    let listener = net::TcpListener::bind("127.0.0.1:0")?;
    io::println("listening on {}", listener.local_addr()?);
    let (client, addr) = listener.accept()?;
    io::println("connection from {}", addr);
    client.write("hello\\n")?;
    Ok(())
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xur.std (7)
